+## Sharing Buffers Without Copying
+
+By default every value crosses by copy: a `Vec<number>` is walked element by
+element onto the MessagePack wire and rebuilt on the other side. For a handful
+of numbers that is nothing. For a million it is most of the call.
+
+A numeric array parameter can instead be **shared** — exported to the foreign
+body as a view over Shape's own memory, with no copy in either direction:
+
+```shape runnable=false
+fn python total(shared xs: Vec<number>) -> Result<number> {
+    return float(sum(xs))
+}
+
+fn python normalise(shared mut xs: Vec<number>) -> Result<int> {
+    top = max(xs)
+    for i in range(len(xs)):
+        xs[i] = xs[i] / top
+    return len(xs)
+}
+```
+
+`shared` gives the body a read-only view. `shared mut` gives it a writable one,
+and the writes land in the caller's array directly — `normalise` returns a count,
+not an array, because the data never left.
+
+In Python a shared parameter arrives as a `memoryview`, not a `list`. It
+indexes and iterates like a sequence and NumPy wraps it without copying:
+
+```shape runnable=false
+fn python correlation(shared xs: Vec<number>, shared ys: Vec<number>) -> Result<number> {
+    import numpy as np
+    return float(np.corrcoef(np.asarray(xs), np.asarray(ys))[0, 1])
+}
+```
+
+### Why you have to ask for it
+
+Sharing is spelled in the source rather than applied by the compiler, because
+it widens what a mistake in a foreign body can do. An ordinary fence can only
+corrupt its own copy of the data; a body holding a shared view past the end of
+the call would be reading Shape's memory after Shape has reused it. Writing
+`shared` is how you take that on, and it is visible to everyone who reads the
+declaration afterwards.
+
+Shape makes the window as small as it can:
+
+- **The view lives exactly as long as the call.** When the body returns, Shape
+  asks Python whether every view was released. If anything still holds one, the
+  call FAILS with a boundary error naming the parameter — before the memory is
+  reused. A body that does `GLOBAL.append(numpy.asarray(xs))` gets an error, not
+  a crash three seconds later somewhere else.
+- **A read-only view really is read-only.** `xs[0] = 1.0` inside a `shared`
+  body raises `TypeError: cannot modify read-only memory`. That is CPython
+  refusing, not Shape checking afterwards.
+- **A stashed view is dead, not dangling.** Shape releases every view on the way
+  out, so if one escaped anyway, reading it raises `ValueError: operation
+  forbidden on released memoryview`.
+
+If you need to keep the data, copy it: `numpy.array(xs)` copies where
+`numpy.asarray(xs)` does not, and `bytes(xs)` or `list(xs)` work too.
+
+### What can be shared
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| `Vec<int>` and `Vec<number>` | shareable — contiguous `i64` / `f64` |
+| `Vec<bool>` | not shareable: one byte per element with only two valid values, so a writable view could store something that is neither `true` nor `false` |
+| `Vec<string>` | not shareable: the array holds pointers into Shape's heap |
+| anything not an array | there is no buffer to share |
+| `fn typescript` | not shareable today — see below |
+| `async fn python` | not shareable: see [Async foreign functions](#async-foreign-functions) |
+
+Everything in that table is a `[C0935]` compile error at the declaration, with
+the reason in the message. `shared` on an ordinary Shape function is one too:
+Shape's own calls never copied an array to pass it, so the word buys nothing
+there.
+
+`fn typescript` declarations cannot share yet. The TypeScript runtime can build
+an `ArrayBuffer` over external memory, but it has no way to tell Shape whether
+the body let go of it, and Shape will not hand out a pointer it cannot ask
+about. A `shared` TypeScript parameter is refused at the call, and the generated
+`.d.ts` types it `never`, so the editor says so first.
+
+`async` and `shared` do not combine. An async foreign call returns to Shape
+immediately and runs the body on a worker thread, so "as long as the call" stops
+meaning anything on the Shape side — the array could be freed while the body is
+still reading it. Drop one keyword or the other.
+
